# Jekyll Blog Guidelines

## Content Rules

- Posts are technical content aimed at backend/systems engineers. Write in a direct, practical tone — no marketing, no fluff.
- Code examples must be realistic and complete enough to be useful. Prefer Java/Spring Boot for backend examples.
- Never invent fake metrics, team names, or company details. Keep scenarios generic but technically accurate.
- Front matter must include: layout, title, date, categories.

## Structure Rules

- All site source lives in `docs/` — never create Jekyll files at the repo root.
- Layouts go in `docs/_layouts/`, includes in `docs/_includes/`, posts in `docs/_posts/`.
- Static assets go in `docs/assets/` (images in `docs/assets/images/`).
- Post filenames follow `YYYY-MM-DD-slug.md` format.

## Style Rules

- Match existing CSS patterns in `docs/assets/main.scss` — don't add new stylesheets.
- The layout uses a three-column CSS grid. Respect the existing breakpoint at 768px.
- Use semantic HTML. No inline styles.
- Font Awesome icons are available via CDN (loaded in `_includes/head.html`).

## Change Discipline

- Touch only what the task requires. Don't "improve" unrelated markup or styles.
- Test locally with `bundle exec jekyll serve` before considering any change complete.
- If adding a new section or component, check if an existing include or layout already handles it.
